-- Backfill cards.series_id / cards.series_name for Pokemon (TCGdex + pokemontcg.io) and
-- One Piece (optcg). Both columns already existed on cards (see column comments) and are
-- already part of MANAGED_FIELDS / buildIncomingFromBrief in scripts/lib/pokemon-sync.js --
-- the live sync pipeline has written them correctly for NEW/incomplete rows since that code
-- landed. The reason ~100% of existing rows were still NULL: scripts/sync-cards.js skips a
-- whole set+lang combination at the SET level (countInDb >= totalCards) before ever calling
-- processSetCards/buildIncomingFromBrief again, so a field added to MANAGED_FIELDS after a
-- set was already "complete" never gets backfilled onto existing rows by a normal sync run.
--
-- This migration documents a backfill that was ALREADY EXECUTED directly against production
-- (project pimwkmwrduqkaydyvxqz) on 2026-08-16 via direct SQL (Supabase MCP), because the
-- data pipeline itself has no standalone "recompute managed fields for already-complete sets"
-- entry point -- only scripts/sync-cards.js's set-level-gated sync. It is captured here for
-- version control / reproducibility, not to be re-run blindly: every statement is guarded by
-- "series_id IS NULL", so re-running this file is a no-op against the current production
-- database (idempotent), and it will only ever backfill true nulls if run again elsewhere
-- (e.g. a fresh environment restored from an older snapshot).
--
-- Does NOT touch canonical_cards, cards.canonical_card_id, or any other cards column --
-- only cards.series_id and cards.series_name, only where they were NULL.
--
-- Sources (provenance):
--   pokemon/tcgdex : GET https://api.tcgdex.net/v2/{lang}/series and .../series/{id}
--                    -- serie.id/serie.name, per language, exactly what
--                    -- scripts/lib/pokemon-sync.js's buildIncomingFromBrief already reads
--                    -- per-set via setData.serie during a normal sync.
--   pokemon/ptcg   : GET https://api.pokemontcg.io/v2/sets -- .series (plain string).
--                    series_id is a slug derived from that string since pokemontcg.io has
--                    no separate series id field.
--   onepiece/optcg : official set-code prefix (OP-/ST-/EB-/PRB-/P/OTHER), matching Bandai's
--                    own product-type categorization visible in the "series" filter at
--                    https://en.onepiece-cardgame.com/cardlist/ (BOOSTER PACK / STARTER DECK /
--                    EXTRA BOOSTER / PREMIUM BOOSTER / Promotion / Other Product). Bandai does
--                    not publish a multi-set "era" grouping the way TCGdex does for Pokemon,
--                    so this product-line categorization is the closest authoritative,
--                    non-invented analog.
--
-- Verified coverage after this backfill (2026-08-16, checked directly against production):
--   pokemon:  154017 cards, 153891 with series_id (99.92%). Remaining 126 NULLs are DE/FR
--             McDonald's Collection sets (2022swsh/2023sv/2024sv on DE; 2018sm/2019sm on FR)
--             that TCGdex does not expose in those languages at all (404 on
--             /v2/{lang}/sets/{id}) -- left NULL rather than invented.
--   onepiece: 5195 cards, 100% covered.
--   mtg/ygo:  untouched (0% before and after), per task scope -- architecture-only TCGs,
--             no active work per CLAUDE.md priority rule.
--
-- No set_id maps to more than one series_id within a given (tcg, source): hierarchy
-- Game -> Series -> Set -> Card is a clean tree; only the localized series_name text varies
-- per language, exactly like set_name already does elsewhere in this table.

-- ============================================================================
-- POKEMON / TCGDEX -- one UPDATE per language. series_id is TCGdex's own short series code
-- (stable across languages); series_name is the localized label for that language, mirroring
-- how cards.set_name is already stored per-language elsewhere in this table.
-- ============================================================================

